# Environment file for docker-compose.yml, which runs the DEMO only.
#
#     PowerShell:  Copy-Item .env.example .env
#     macOS/Linux: cp .env.example .env
#
# Docker Compose reads .env from this folder automatically. .env is gitignored:
# never commit it, and never put a real secret in this .example file.
#
# This is the only variable docker-compose.yml reads. The others belong elsewhere:
#   - running the backend without Docker:  backend/.env.example
#   - running the frontend without Docker: frontend/.env.example
# The demo's database name, user and password are fixed inside docker-compose.yml
# itself (a container-only "bloodlink_demo" database of fictional data).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BLOODLINK_JWT_SECRET  (REQUIRED; Compose refuses to start without it)
# The key that signs every login token. Anyone who knows it can forge a login for
# ANY account, administrators included. At least 32 bytes; 48 random bytes in
# Base64 (64 characters) is recommended. The README's "Setting a real JWT secret"
# section shows how to generate one in PowerShell (or: openssl rand -base64 48).
#
# The value below is the PUBLIC PLACEHOLDER. It is in this repository, so everyone
# can read it, and it is accepted ONLY because the demo runs the "demo" profile:
# the backend starts but prints a large warning. Under any other profile it refuses
# to start (Business Rule 15). Replace it with your own value in your .env copy.
#
# A BLOODLINK_JWT_SECRET already set in your environment (for example permanently,
# as the README describes) takes precedence over this file. Changing the secret
# signs every user out: tokens signed with the old one stop working.
#
# Keep this line identical to the default in application.properties.example:
# JwtSecretPolicyTest fails the build if the two ever differ.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BLOODLINK_JWT_SECRET=local-dev-only-change-me-0123456789abcdef
